      "question": [
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "376415",
          "text": "If we have a linear probe hash table of size $\\text{M}=30,$ and a cluster of size $\\text{C}=14,$ the probability that a random key will be inserted into the location immediately after the cluster is __________.",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "0.5",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_ds_tw5,numerical-answers,goclasses,algorithms,hashing,linear-probing,two-marks",
          "category": "Algorithms"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "406839",
          "text": "<p>Suppose we use a hash function $h$ to hash $n$ distinct keys into an array $\\text{T}$ of length $m$. Say that two distinct keys $x, y$ collide under $h$ if $h(x)=h(y)$. Assuming simple uniform hashing - that is, with each key mapped independently and uniformly to a random bucket - what is the probability that a given pair $x, y$ of distinct keys collide?</p>\n\n<ol class=\"shrink-inline-options\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$\\dfrac{1}{m-1}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\dfrac{1}{m}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\dfrac{1}{n^2}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\dfrac{1}{m^2}$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_ds_tw5,goclasses,algorithms,hashing,uniform-hashing,probability,one-mark",
          "category": "Algorithms"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "406837",
          "text": "<p>Let we insert the values $74,924,83,113$, and $5$ in the given order into a hash table of size $10,$ using the hash function $k&nbsp;\\% 10$ and resolving collisions with quadratic probing. Determine which items we would encounter in order to search for the value $65?$</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$924$</li>\n\t<li>$5$</li>\n\t<li>$83$</li>\n\t<li>$113$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "a;b",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_ds_tw5,goclasses,algorithms,hashing,quadratic-probing,multiple-selects,one-mark",
          "category": "Algorithms"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "376417",
          "text": "<p>Suppose $k$ randomly selected keys are hashed into the range $[0 \\ldots n-1]$ using a uniformly distributed hashing function. Assume that $k \\leq n$. What is the probability that all $k$ keys yield distinct hash values (so no collisions occur)?</p>\n\n<ol class=\"shrink-inline-options\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$\\dfrac{(n-1) !}{(n-k) ! n^{k-1}}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\dfrac{(n-1) !}{(n-(k-1)) ! n^{k}}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\dfrac{n !(k-1)}{(n-(k-1)) ! n^{k}}$</li>\n\t<li>None of these</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_ds_tw5,goclasses,algorithms,hashing,uniform-hashing,one-mark",
          "category": "Algorithms"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "376404",
          "text": "<p>Suppose you insert three keys into a hash table with $m$ slots. Assuming the simple uniform hashing assumption, and given that collisions are resolved by chaining, what is the probability that both slots $0$ and $1$ are empty?</p>\n\n<ol class=\"shrink-inline-options\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$((m-2) / m)^ 3$</li>\n\t<li>$((m-3) / m)^ 3$</li>\n\t<li>$(m / m-1)^ 3$</li>\n\t<li>$((m-2) /(m-1))^ 3$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_ds_tw5,goclasses,algorithms,hashing,uniform-hashing,two-marks",
          "category": "Algorithms"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "406834",
          "text": "Given a hashing table of $10$ entries, the hash function $h(x)=x \\bmod (10)$ and the input sequence\n$$\\{71,23,73,99,44,79,89\\}$$\nWhat will be the load factor of the hash table when collisions are resolved with open addressing and linear probing?",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "0.7",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_ds_tw5,numerical-answers,goclasses,algorithms,hashing,linear-probing,two-marks",
          "category": "Algorithms"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "376411",
          "text": "<p>Consider a hash table of $9$ slots implemented with linear probing. Suppose we insert $2$ elements in a sequence to a hash table with a simple uniform hashing assumption. What is the probability that we end up with $2$ consecutive slots of the hash table filled?<br>\n<br>\nSlot $i$ and $(i+1) \\text{mod m}$ are defined to be consecutive slots.</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$1/2$</li>\n\t<li>$1/3$</li>\n\t<li>$1/4$</li>\n\t<li>$2/3$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "b",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_ds_tw5,goclasses,algorithms,hashing,linear-probing,two-marks",
          "category": "Algorithms"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "376406",
          "text": "<p>An open-addressing hash table(with $m$ slots)&nbsp;that resolves collisions using linear probing is initially empty. Key $k 1$ is inserted into the table first, followed by $k 2,$ and then $k 3$ (the keys themselves are drawn randomly from a universal set of keys).</p>\n\n<p>What is the probability that searching for $k 1$ takes exactly two probes?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$(m-2) / m)^ 3$</li>\n\t<li>$3 / m^ 2$</li>\n\t<li>$2 / m^ 2$</li>\n\t<li>None of these</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_ds_tw5,goclasses,algorithms,hashing,linear-probing,two-marks",
          "category": "Algorithms"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "376413",
          "text": "Assuming the simple uniform hashing is implemented into a hash table with $m= 3$ slots. What is the probability for a key to map to slot $1$ given that key does not map to slot $0?$",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "0.5",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_ds_tw5,numerical-answers,goclasses,algorithms,hashing,uniform-hashing,one-mark",
          "category": "Algorithms"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "376402",
          "text": "<p>Consider a hash table with $m$ slots that uses chaining for collision resolution. The table is initially empty. What is the probability that after $4$ keys are inserted that at least a chain of size $3$ is created?</p>\n\n<ol class=\"shrink-inline-options\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$\\dfrac{4 m-3}{m^{3}}$</li>\n\t<li>$m^{-4}$</li>\n\t<li>$m^{-3}(m-1)$</li>\n\t<li>$m^{-2}$</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_ds_tw5,goclasses,algorithms,hashing,chaining,two-marks",
          "category": "Algorithms"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "376392",
          "text": "<p>Consider a hash table with $n$ buckets, where external (overflow) Chaining is used to resolve collisions. The hash function is such that the probability that a key value is hashed to a particular bucket is $1/n $.</p>\n\n<p>The hash table is initially empty and $k$ distinct values are inserted in the table. What is the probability that the first collision occurs at the $k^{\\text{th}}$&nbsp;insertions?</p>\n\n<ol class=\"shrink-inline-options\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$\\dfrac{(n-1) !}{(n-k) ! \\;n^{k-1}}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\dfrac{(n-1) !}{(n-(k-1)) ! \\;n^{k}}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\dfrac{n !(k-1)}{(n-(k-1)) ! \\;n^{k}}$</li>\n\t<li>None of these</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "c",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.6666",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_ds_tw5,goclasses,algorithms,hashing,chaining,two-marks",
          "category": "Algorithms"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "376421",
          "text": "Consider an open-address hash table with uniform hashing. What is the minimum integer upper bound on the expected number of probes in an unsuccessful search when the load factor is $3 / 4$.",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "4",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_ds_tw5,numerical-answers,goclasses,algorithms,hashing,uniform-hashing,two-marks",
          "category": "Algorithms"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "376419",
          "text": "<p>Suppose $\\mathrm{M}$ randomly selected keys are hashed into the range $[0\\ldots&nbsp;\\text{N}-1]$ using a uniformly distributed hashing function. What is the probability that all $\\text{M}$ keys yield the same hash value (so all $\\text{M}$ keys collide at the same slot$)?$</p>\n\n<ol class=\"shrink-inline-options\" style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>$\\dfrac{1}{\\text{N}^{\\text{M}-1}}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\dfrac{1}{\\text{N}^{\\text{M}}}$</li>\n\t<li>$\\dfrac{\\text{M}}{\\text{N}^{\\text{M}}}$</li>\n\t<li>None of these</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Choice",
          "answer": "a",
          "award": 1,
          "penalty": "0.3333",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_ds_tw5,goclasses,algorithms,hashing,uniform-hashing,one-mark",
          "category": "Algorithms"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "380819",
          "text": "<p>Which of the following is is/are true?</p>\n\n<ol style=\"list-style-type:upper-alpha\">\n\t<li>After hashing $n$ keys into a hash table of size $m$ that uses chaining to handle collisions, we hash two new keys $k_{1}$ and $k_{2}$. Under the simple uniform hashing assumption, the probability that $k_{1}$ and $k_{2}$ are hashed into the same table location is exactly $1 / m$ with no dependence on the number of keys $n$.</li>\n\t<li>Under the uniform hashing assumption, if we use a hash table of size $m$ with open addressing to hash $3$ keys, the probability that the third inserted key needs exactly three probes before being inserted into the table is exactly $\\frac{2}{m(m-1)}$.</li>\n\t<li>We use a hash table of size $m$ with open addressing to hash $n$ items. Under the uniform hashing assumption, the expected cost to insert another element into the table is at most $1+\\alpha$, where $\\alpha=n / m$ is the average load.</li>\n\t<li>Linear probing is equivalent to double hashing with a secondary hash function of $h_{2}(k)=1$.</li>\n</ol>",
          "type": "Multiple Select",
          "answer": "a;b;d",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_ds_tw5,goclasses,algorithms,hashing,uniform-hashing,linear-probing,multiple-selects,two-marks",
          "category": "Data Structures"
        },
        {
          "contents": "",
          "post_id": "376398",
          "text": "<p>Suppose we implement linear probing with the hash function $h(x) = x \\mod 9.$ After inserting $7$ keys, the table is shown as below:<br>\n<strong><img alt=\"\" height=\"121\" src=\"data:image/png;base64,\"></strong><br>\nHow many total insertion orders of keys are possible to reach the above state of the hash table?</p>\n\n<p>For example, two possible insertion orders are -</p>\n\n<ul>\n\t<li>$12, 14, 3, 9, 4, 18, 21$ and</li>\n\t<li>$9, 12, 14, 3, 4, 21, 18$</li>\n</ul>",
          "type": "Numerical",
          "answer": "63",
          "award": 2,
          "penalty": "0.0000",
          "tags": "goclasses_cs_ds_tw5,numerical-answers,goclasses,algorithms,hashing,linear-probing,two-marks",
          "category": "Algorithms"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}